{"causes": ["The flourishing of medical universities in Bologna, Montpellier, and Paris during the High Middle Ages provided advanced educational opportunities for aspiring physicians and surgeons.", "The rediscovery and translation of classical Greco-Roman and Arabic medical texts, especially those by Galen and Avicenna, stimulated renewed interest in anatomy and surgical theory.", "A growing demand for skilled practitioners in an era of frequent warfare and urban growth highlighted the need for standardized surgical knowledge and comprehensive training."], "effects": ["Henri de Mondeville's treatise *La Chirurgie* became a foundational text that significantly advanced the theoretical and practical understanding of medieval surgery.", "His advocacy for primary wound closure without suppuration profoundly influenced subsequent approaches to wound management, challenging centuries-old medical dogma.", "Mondeville's detailed anatomical descriptions and use of illustrations helped to establish a precedent for integrating visual aids and empirical observation into medical education."], "relationships": [{"sourceSlug": "henri-de-mondeville", "sourceName": "Henri de Mondeville", "verb": "CREATES", "targetSlug": "la-chirurgie", "targetName": "La Chirurgie", "context": "Henri de Mondeville authored *La Chirurgie*, the first comprehensive surgical treatise written in French between 1306 and 1320."}, {"sourceSlug": "henri-de-mondeville", "sourceName": "Henri de Mondeville", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "guy-de-chauliac", "targetName": "Guy de Chauliac", "context": "Henri de Mondeville's surgical theories and practices significantly influenced Guy de Chauliac, a prominent 14th-century surgeon who referenced Mondeville's work."}, {"sourceSlug": "henri-de-mondeville", "sourceName": "Henri de Mondeville", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "paris-france", "targetName": "Paris, France", "context": "Henri de Mondeville taught and practiced medicine in Paris, France, serving as a royal surgeon and contributing to the University of Paris's medical faculty."}, {"sourceSlug": "medieval-medical-universities", "sourceName": "Medieval Medical Universities", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "henri-de-mondeville", "targetName": "Henri de Mondeville", "context": "Medieval medical universities, particularly those in Bologna and Montpellier, provided Henri de Mondeville with the advanced anatomical and surgical education that shaped his career."}, {"sourceSlug": "henri-de-mondeville", "sourceName": "Henri de Mondeville", "verb": "TRANSMITS", "targetSlug": "classical-anatomical-knowledge", "targetName": "Classical Anatomical Knowledge", "context": "Henri de Mondeville transmitted and elaborated upon classical anatomical knowledge, particularly from Galen, through his teachings and written works."}], "places": [{"name": "Paris, France", "role": "Academic Center"}, {"name": "Montpellier, France", "role": "Medical School"}, {"name": "Bologna, Italy", "role": "Anatomical Study"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Henri de Mondeville", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "henri-de-mondeville"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q712736", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Henri_de_Mondeville"], "tags": ["Physicians & Medical Pioneers", "innovation-and-technology", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Henri de Mondeville's *La Chirurgie* established a new standard for surgical texts in Europe, introducing innovative wound care practices and influencing generations of surgeons, most notably Guy de Chauliac.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-21T20:17:28Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44944676320", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Henri de Mondeville was a medieval French surgeon who made a significant number of contributions to anatomy and surgery, and was the first Frenchman to author a surgical treatise, La Chirurgie (1306-1320). Very little is known about the details of his early life. There is some doubt about his birthplace as according to ancient Norman custom, his last name is derived from the place of birth, and is variously spelled as Amondeville, Esmondeville, Mandeville and so on.", "newValue": "Henri de Mondeville, a pivotal French surgeon of the early 14th century, profoundly advanced surgical practice and anatomical understanding through his seminal work, *La Chirurgie* (1306-1320). As the first Frenchman to author a comprehensive surgical treatise, Mondeville synthesized classical knowledge with contemporary observations, challenging prevailing medical dogmas and advocating for rational, antiseptic wound care. His work emerged during a period of burgeoning medical education in Europe, particularly at centers like the University of Montpellier and the University of Paris, where he taught and practiced.

Mondeville's most significant contributions include his advocacy for primary wound closure without the formation of pus (a radical departure from the common belief that pus was necessary for healing), meticulous anatomical descriptions, and the integration of illustrations into surgical texts. He studied anatomy extensively in Bologna, then the leading center for anatomical dissection, and later served as a royal surgeon to King Philip IV and King Louis X of France. His treatise detailed surgical procedures for fractures, dislocations, hernia repair, and cataract removal, emphasizing hygiene, careful dissection, and pain management through early forms of anesthesia.

The legacy of Henri de Mondeville reverberated through subsequent generations of European surgeons, most notably influencing Guy de Chauliac, whose own influential *Chirurgia Magna* drew heavily upon Mondeville's innovations. His emphasis on direct observation, anatomical precision, and rational wound management laid foundational principles that would gradually displace superstitious and less effective practices, contributing to the professionalization and scientific grounding of surgery. Mondeville's pioneering efforts helped transition surgery from a craft often relegated to barbers and itinerants towards a respected medical discipline grounded in empirical knowledge."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-21T20:17:28Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44944676320", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "1260, Normandy"}, {"title": "Medical challenges driving innovation in healing arts", "type": "Idea", "year": "1260, Normandy"}], "newValue": ["The flourishing of medical universities in Bologna, Montpellier, and Paris during the High Middle Ages provided advanced educational opportunities for aspiring physicians and surgeons.", "The rediscovery and translation of classical Greco-Roman and Arabic medical texts, especially those by Galen and Avicenna, stimulated renewed interest in anatomy and surgical theory.", "A growing demand for skilled practitioners in an era of frequent warfare and urban growth highlighted the need for standardized surgical knowledge and comprehensive training."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-21T20:17:28Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#44944676320", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Improved medical practice and public health outcomes", "type": "Idea", "year": "1320"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "ty